  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is a Gamma distribution with shape parameter $a + \\sum x_i$ and rate parameter $n + \\beta$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:17:17\u201303:17:47]: 'so we just add xi we just add b plus n and other things are constant so in fact we showed that our p lambda conditioned on x because yeah i here wrote some not very beautiful things we can show better that it's proportional to exponent minus n plus b lambda and lambda a plus some xi minus one.' This indicates the posterior is proportional to $\\lambda^{(a + \\sum x_i) - 1} e^{-(n + \\beta)\\lambda}$, which is the form of a Gamma distribution with shape $a + \\sum x_i$ and rate $n + \\beta$ (where $n$ is the number of observations). The speaker explicitly states the parameter updates: 'a n equals to a plus some, y' (where 'y' is likely $\\sum x_i$) and 'b and equals to b plus n' (where 'n' is the number of observations). The final statement at [03:18:19\u201303:18:48] confirms the posterior is a Gamma distribution with these updated parameters."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The joint probability of Poisson observations given $\\lambda$ is $\\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{e^{-\\lambda} \\lambda^{x_i}}{x_i!}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:12:08\u201303:12:43]: 'or just product of P x i conditioned from lambda. This is Poisson distribution, Poisson density, this is prior density and on top is again reduction of x i conditioned from lambda.' The speaker states the joint distribution is the product of Poisson densities for each $X_i$ given $\\lambda$."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is proportional to $\\lambda^{(a + \\sum x_i) - 1} e^{-(n + \\beta)\\lambda}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:14:10\u201303:15:29]: 'So, in fact, what we have? Finally, we have exponent minus lambda n. Yes, exponent minus lambda n. Lambda sum xi. Then, product of factorial collapse, gamma function, and b in the a degree collapses. Also, lambda i minus 1 exponent minus b lambda.' This leads to the expression $\\lambda^{(a + \\sum x_i) - 1} e^{-(n + \\beta)\\lambda}$ as the posterior proportional to this form."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The prior distribution for $\\lambda$ is a Gamma distribution with shape $a$ and rate $\\beta$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:13:30\u201303:14:10]: 'It is of the A divided by gamma function on A, and A minus one, exponent minus B lambda.' This describes the Gamma prior as $\\frac{\\beta^{a} \\lambda^{a-1} e^{-\\beta \\lambda}}{\\Gamma(a)}$."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution has a shape parameter equal to the prior shape plus the sum of observed counts $\\sum x_i$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:17:17\u201303:17:47]: 'so we just add xi we just add b plus n and other things are constant so in fact we showed that our p lambda conditioned on x because yeah i here wrote some not very beautiful things we can show better that it's proportional to exponent minus n plus b lambda and lambda a plus some xi minus one.' The speaker explicitly states that the shape parameter is updated by adding $\\sum x_i$ to the prior shape $a$."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution has a rate parameter equal to the number of observations $n$ plus the prior rate $\\beta$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:17:17\u201303:17:47]: 'b and equals to b plus n' (where 'b' is the prior rate $\\beta$ and 'n' is the number of observations). The speaker states that the rate parameter is updated by adding $n$ to the prior rate $\\beta$."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is a Gamma distribution with the same form as the prior but with updated parameters",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:18:19\u201303:18:48]: 'we can compute that the denominator integral of this is exactly the inverse constant. So our real distribution just multiplied on this constant. And that's why it's like a trick how to easily compute real distribution with Bau's method.' The speaker confirms the posterior is a Gamma distribution with updated parameters, similar to the prior form."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The joint distribution simplifies to a Gamma distribution when the prior and likelihood are combined",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:14:10\u201303:15:29]: 'So, in fact, what we have? Finally, we have exponent minus lambda n. Yes, exponent minus lambda n. Lambda sum xi. Then, product of factorial collapse, gamma function, and b in the a degree collapses.' The speaker describes the joint distribution simplifying to a Gamma distribution form with updated parameters."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 3,
  "caveat": "The ASR has some speech errors and unclear phrasing (e.g., 'b and equals to b plus n' might be misinterpreted as 'beta and equals to beta plus n'), but the key claims about the posterior distribution being Gamma with updated parameters are clearly supported by the timestamps provided."
